---
description: Dependency lifetimes and construction
globs: ["libs/*/src/**/*.ts", "libs/*/src/**/*.tsx", "apps/server/src/**/*.ts"]
alwaysApply: true
---

Three layers. Do not blur them.

1. Primitives (`SqlClient`, `Clock`, `createId`) are the only long-lived
   dependencies. Thread them through request context / `ClientEnv` / `ServerEnv`.
   Never build a container/registry object. Do not introduce an `Io` bag.

2. Use-case classes take primitives in the constructor: `(db: SqlClient, clock)`
   or `(db, { clock, createId })`. One class per endpoint/use case. Write methods
   open `db.transaction`. Read methods construct Layer C with the request `db` as
   `SqlTx` without a write transaction. Use cases must not call other use cases.

3. Repositories, services, publishers, and handlers take `SqlTx` (never
   `SqlClient`) plus `clock` / `createId` as needed. `SqlTx` is
   `Pick<SqlClient, 'query' | 'run'>` from `@pkgs/sql-client/interface`. Construct
   them inline inside a `db.transaction(async (tx) => …)` callback (writes) or at
   the point of use (reads). Never store one on a field of anything longer-lived
   than that callback. Never construct one eagerly "in case it's needed."

If wiring repeats across use cases, extract a factory FUNCTION taking
`(tx, clock)` — never a class that holds constructed objects.

Read `now` per-operation via `clock.now()`; don't capture it at construction.

Do not convert `defineMutation` / `defineQuery` `run` handlers, React hooks, or
`@io-wall-ok` composition roots. Inside mutations, `new XRepository(tx)` is
correct.

CI: `ci:no-top-level-di-functions`, `ci:no-eager-di-construction`,
`ci:layer-c-sql-tx`; Biome `no-di-container.grit` (via `ci:lint`).

Full rationale: docs/dependency-injection.md
